he action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9eace5df-6443-5362-a150-ffc1f660fca5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9eace5df-6443-5362-a150-ffc1f660fca5" :answer-field/id #uuid "9eace5df-6443-5362-a150-ffc1f660fca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b7275d43-89fa-5c4e-87da-2ba55e190ef7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-712fd56c-b9b0-5f98-ae65-a16f738b67c7" "answer-2d13a6f3-9009-51cd-9101-1cc9ebe6bb52" "answer-f316d8aa-f206-5c87-8850-b87ffed7c21d" "answer-b7275d43-89fa-5c4e-87da-2ba55e190ef7" "answer-f9bbd151-4ca2-5dfd-8018-cf7ce67dc55f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-712fd56c-b9b0-5f98-ae65-a16f738b67c7" :answer/id #uuid "712fd56c-b9b0-5f98-ae65-a16f738b67c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d13a6f3-9009-51cd-9101-1cc9ebe6bb52" :answer/id #uuid "2d13a6f3-9009-51cd-9101-1cc9ebe6bb52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f316d8aa-f206-5c87-8850-b87ffed7c21d" :answer/id #uuid "f316d8aa-f206-5c87-8850-b87ffed7c21d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7275d43-89fa-5c4e-87da-2ba55e190ef7" :answer/id #uuid "b7275d43-89fa-5c4e-87da-2ba55e190ef7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9bbd151-4ca2-5dfd-8018-cf7ce67dc55f" :answer/id #uuid "f9bbd151-4ca2-5dfd-8018-cf7ce67dc55f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125230 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B},$ given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123980 :answer-field/correct "answer-00eb9f24-6f46-54fd-881a-8d0acb355862" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5a9161b-1f34-52ee-845b-e2920c4d6d07" "answer-00eb9f24-6f46-54fd-881a-8d0acb355862" "answer-6965b1d4-ff01-556f-9f85-99fc64ff52a2" "answer-7ffa4ce2-47ab-5b1a-8640-84c2a9d980c0" "answer-fbd3c63c-9bc4-5c04-a42e-f628e22e119a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5a9161b-1f34-52ee-845b-e2920c4d6d07" :answer/id #uuid "d5a9161b-1f34-52ee-845b-e2920c4d6d07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00eb9f24-6f46-54fd-881a-8d0acb355862" :answer/id #uuid "00eb9f24-6f46-54fd-881a-8d0acb355862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6965b1d4-ff01-556f-9f85-99fc64ff52a2" :answer/id #uuid "6965b1d4-ff01-556f-9f85-99fc64ff52a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ffa4ce2-47ab-5b1a-8640-84c2a9d980c0" :answer/id #uuid "7ffa4ce2-47ab-5b1a-8640-84c2a9d980c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd3c63c-9bc4-5c04-a42e-f628e22e119a" :answer/id #uuid "fbd3c63c-9bc4-5c04-a42e-f628e22e119a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323973 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=8{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186392245 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125121 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=3{b}_{2}-4{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326726 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=8{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=7{b}_{1}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416398 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}.$ The matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & b & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351427 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -11\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -24 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -24 \\\\ 5 & -11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366026 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -7 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -18 & -46 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125122 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-071d0b70-e887-5685-aa09-942aa6ae2d26" :question/id #uuid "071d0b70-e887-5685-aa09-942aa6ae2d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-82477" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe0f5a4d-5c79-5a6e-821d-dd222e30da56"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe0f5a4d-5c79-5a6e-821d-dd222e30da56" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0f5a4d-5c79-5a6e-821d-dd222e30da56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66345c2f-408a-546e-8bbe-cfe5bd0741cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28b5da98-7bce-5e58-b801-a5f1d94c1ce4" "answer-b4f993f6-06f9-5abf-85e2-3e17d78dc19b" "answer-121c587a-8335-5d2c-aee8-0d725d16bf3f" "answer-66345c2f-408a-546e-8bbe-cfe5bd0741cd" "answer-ae0299d8-4cd8-5abc-bdcb-7ad0a5cbd0a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28b5da98-7bce-5e58-b801-a5f1d94c1ce4" :answer/id #uuid "28b5da98-7bce-5e58-b801-a5f1d94c1ce4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4f993f6-06f9-5abf-85e2-3e17d78dc19b" :answer/id #uuid "b4f993f6-06f9-5abf-85e2-3e17d78dc19b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-121c587a-8335-5d2c-aee8-0d725d16bf3f" :answer/id #uuid "121c587a-8335-5d2c-aee8-0d725d16bf3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66345c2f-408a-546e-8bbe-cfe5bd0741cd" :answer/id #uuid "66345c2f-408a-546e-8bbe-cfe5bd0741cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae0299d8-4cd8-5abc-bdcb-7ad0a5cbd0a3" :answer/id #uuid "ae0299d8-4cd8-5abc-bdcb-7ad0a5cbd0a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ -7 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394387 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125143 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & b & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x-y \\\\ x-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -3 & -1 & -10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(-2)+(-2)=-1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351432 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 & c \\\\ -1 & b & -3 \\\\ a & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y-x \\\\ y+z-x\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 & -2 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-3,$ $b=1,$ $c=19$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+1+19=17.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351428 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & b & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2})=-1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416397 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351431 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & b & -25 \\\\ a & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}y-z \\\\ x+y-z \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 & 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=3,$ $c=7$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+3+7=10.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125797 :tutorial/content "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n![](images/f8/f8e3c07c0f636b0c144a786bddf25531c53a8becdbd31d5662c40e890f1762e7.svg)\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n![](images/5f/5fa7935eb183be7d71c2193a4f6f915b244da09baf9313481c1a99f51b12fc06.svg)\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n![](images/56/5659e7bcc3f941bde2c14eed5770a4af941eb330012dee8c641b099f51000a67.svg)\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation ![](images/5d/5da1bda6e75a1505bb9c83c257f8000c8bb9f803c6408792ff06a6ff5f97f4fe.svg), with respect to the basis \n\n![](images/c8/c81fbb505e2031f3c5e1f51ec1d5075a8fa48d7aa2885f6a2cfb3ba9680e59bb.svg)\n\nwe have \n\n![](images/98/988515526eac83d57b73bedf45b675a583ee97240e58771b7c3f2a601b230048.svg)\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n![](images/88/8801d7d3a19c5c780a0d18a0e1cf5010e2c84d8213f884173e85c6d8766eb3b0.svg)\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n![](images/cb/cbb4e2be1df2dca94359dfb2b7c505eeef5829a18e38355536edb2ff7a64e587.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n![](images/6f/6f49f2dbd51e372c14ced80b1d1268c0f3e184f0ebbb3a24c43bdba9de6917c9.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125798 :tutorial/content "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently ![](images/51/510ee501a49036caf8920d580f9bf7f44de1a6a8704fc6bd5c5edd803db9a2e8.svg).\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n![](images/25/2551ca0145f3fdec0011109ab101dbd2540d55fae25902724be67a65de8b83f8.svg)\n\nTherefore\n![](images/5b/5b6bac88aa9b19c3c3d300a34c1e587162cfb2c9782a4da3f52b1a1322dd222b.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124746 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087777 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164" :question/id #uuid "2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164" :question/math-academy-id "q-64691" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cdc5453e-43b0-5db5-af2d-6f25e95cf391"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cdc5453e-43b0-5db5-af2d-6f25e95cf391" :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc5453e-43b0-5db5-af2d-6f25e95cf391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5d807d68-7dd1-5e64-a6c9-60f86d530db1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-273613c1-7c87-51dc-8032-d46e680c50a4" "answer-9f6f7437-a625-52be-82dd-a8fe85b8d19f" "answer-3d4cc1a3-5fc9-57b5-8c0b-aee4588c2693" "answer-10569fcb-7e9f-575e-9509-740ee2036a5e" "answer-5d807d68-7dd1-5e64-a6c9-60f86d530db1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-273613c1-7c87-51dc-8032-d46e680c50a4" :answer/id #uuid "273613c1-7c87-51dc-8032-d46e680c50a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f6f7437-a625-52be-82dd-a8fe85b8d19f" :answer/id #uuid "9f6f7437-a625-52be-82dd-a8fe85b8d19f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d4cc1a3-5fc9-57b5-8c0b-aee4588c2693" :answer/id #uuid "3d4cc1a3-5fc9-57b5-8c0b-aee4588c2693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10569fcb-7e9f-575e-9509-740ee2036a5e" :answer/id #uuid "10569fcb-7e9f-575e-9509-740ee2036a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d807d68-7dd1-5e64-a6c9-60f86d530db1" :answer/id #uuid "5d807d68-7dd1-5e64-a6c9-60f86d530db1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087776 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-217cbddd-95c6-5476-9ab1-c973151ef34b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-217cbddd-95c6-5476-9ab1-c973151ef34b" :question/id #uuid "217cbddd-95c6-5476-9ab1-c973151ef34b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64695" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+100y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 10\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{10}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-916dd54d-8397-5580-93c9-13216913749d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-916dd54d-8397-5580-93c9-13216913749d" :answer-field/id #uuid "916dd54d-8397-5580-93c9-13216913749d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a5b5fcd-a29c-562a-bf8b-1f75f85c8212" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e4da9eb-58ac-5ffd-8391-5239e3f08137" "answer-4d1fee01-a9cc-5f62-9f84-82cfd509796a" "answer-2a5b5fcd-a29c-562a-bf8b-1f75f85c8212" "answer-45e6c364-109b-5f21-b91f-470d8f1c14dc" "answer-ae0e539b-1e11-52de-8ca7-413cfb665f15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e4da9eb-58ac-5ffd-8391-5239e3f08137" :answer/id #uuid "6e4da9eb-58ac-5ffd-8391-5239e3f08137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d1fee01-a9cc-5f62-9f84-82cfd509796a" :answer/id #uuid "4d1fee01-a9cc-5f62-9f84-82cfd509796a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a5b5fcd-a29c-562a-bf8b-1f75f85c8212" :answer/id #uuid "2a5b5fcd-a29c-562a-bf8b-1f75f85c8212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45e6c364-109b-5f21-b91f-470d8f1c14dc" :answer/id #uuid "45e6c364-109b-5f21-b91f-470d8f1c14dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae0e539b-1e11-52de-8ca7-413cfb665f15" :answer/id #uuid "ae0e539b-1e11-52de-8ca7-413cfb665f15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a377c8fc-71a6-5b1e-b3f7-2c9b112bec57" :activity/id #uuid "a377c8fc-71a6-5b1e-b3f7-2c9b112bec57" :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048289 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-06948950-1b74-5ba9-8396-c217d7f75455" "step-ff6cb89c-b94f-558e-a5a4-97618d860b31" "step-56c91c62-cd7d-583e-8106-f750dde05e74" "step-94fe629c-0044-5dc0-9a0f-8a48b281c0d9" "step-d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff"] :activity/first-step "step-06948950-1b74-5ba9-8396-c217d7f75455" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-06948950-1b74-5ba9-8396-c217d7f75455" :step/id #uuid "06948950-1b74-5ba9-8396-c217d7f75455" :step/content 17592186348068 :step/next "step-ff6cb89c-b94f-558e-a5a4-97618d860b31" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff6cb89c-b94f-558e-a5a4-97618d860b31" :step/id #uuid "ff6cb89c-b94f-558e-a5a4-97618d860b31" :step/content "question-2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164" :step/next "step-56c91c62-cd7d-583e-8106-f750dde05e74" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-56c91c62-cd7d-583e-8106-f750dde05e74" :step/id #uuid "56c91c62-cd7d-583e-8106-f750dde05e74" :step/content "question-217cbddd-95c6-5476-9ab1-c973151ef34b" :step/next "step-94fe629c-0044-5dc0-9a0f-8a48b281c0d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-94fe629c-0044-5dc0-9a0f-8a48b281c0d9" :step/id #uuid "94fe629c-0044-5dc0-9a0f-8a48b281c0d9" :step/content 17592186357482 :step/next "step-d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff" :step/id #uuid "d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff" :step/content 17592186265886 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133773 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0502cc65-db2e-5570-9171-0d7053693770"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133582 :question/problem "Consider the vector ![](images/a1/a143c04731aab261451f29291a01d4d15cc42b6042d0df00a5e5a474a2ca1061.svg) Given that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=-12$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v}\\Vert =4,$ what is the smallest possible value of $k?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133774 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9369c09e-6bcc-5680-9471-b4a602dad377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133583 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vectors ![](images/a9/a90f3b03152a6e3dee2d0b4853fc411d946c67a374a9819bd3f5eaf8573db851.svg) and ![](images/7b/7b05ec66921317711d4ba676e77f567b18efe1580074ced7a73ce0fc0e3a2348.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133816 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9155dd9-42f9-5296-ab8b-8f9a62ed1901" "question-b814c1fe-b3ea-5814-8155-d4d3b5c1b767"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134218 :question/problem "Given that $‖u‖=4$ and $u\\cdot v=-16$, find the smallest possible value of $‖2v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-16| & \\le 4\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 4 & \\le 2\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖2v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖2v‖$ is $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131347 :answer-field/correct 17592186131348 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-0502cc65-db2e-5570-9171-0d7053693770" :question/id #uuid "0502cc65-db2e-5570-9171-0d7053693770" :question/math-academy-id "q-40883" :question/problem "Given that $‖u‖=5$ and $u\\cdot v=-30$, find the smallest possible value of $‖3v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-30| & \\le 5\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 6 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 6 & \\le 3\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 18 & \\le ‖3v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖3v‖$ is $18.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85870cc2-890e-5529-ad2f-e4ee9e1292eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85870cc2-890e-5529-ad2f-e4ee9e1292eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "85870cc2-890e-5529-ad2f-e4ee9e1292eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ae04062-c462-523f-beac-423fd05ec480" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c23f382f-6abb-5999-9c29-ee358fe8f502" "answer-0ae04062-c462-523f-beac-423fd05ec480" "answer-f67c2a00-a5c6-59bc-b0f4-04eed1da4800" "answer-4b25f102-eb7c-5dce-bcbf-2d260be92c5a" "answer-3fb2f57b-1605-50b0-b3e8-f0bef71d70ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c23f382f-6abb-5999-9c29-ee358fe8f502" :answer/id #uuid "c23f382f-6abb-5999-9c29-ee358fe8f502" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ae04062-c462-523f-beac-423fd05ec480" :answer/id #uuid "0ae04062-c462-523f-beac-423fd05ec480" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f67c2a00-a5c6-59bc-b0f4-04eed1da4800" :answer/id #uuid "f67c2a00-a5c6-59bc-b0f4-04eed1da4800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b25f102-eb7c-5dce-bcbf-2d260be92c5a" :answer/id #uuid "4b25f102-eb7c-5dce-bcbf-2d260be92c5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fb2f57b-1605-50b0-b3e8-f0bef71d70ab" :answer/id #uuid "3fb2f57b-1605-50b0-b3e8-f0bef71d70ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9369c09e-6bcc-5680-9471-b4a602dad377" :question/id #uuid "9369c09e-6bcc-5680-9471-b4a602dad377" :question/math-academy-id "q-140568" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{140}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}})≈{75.31}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0dc4cf74-ec7a-5064-a626-43941e3de50c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0dc4cf74-ec7a-5064-a626-43941e3de50c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc4cf74-ec7a-5064-a626-43941e3de50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f2d3fee-96ee-53a9-be0e-e1fbe16389df" "answer-a5f880ae-7e88-52b7-a206-237ab0ac051c" "answer-e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" "answer-05d03192-075e-55e0-bdfc-5e2f0694c8cb" "answer-4fa9dd91-9cae-5db7-9b32-261b4c37d3a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f2d3fee-96ee-53a9-be0e-e1fbe16389df" :answer/id #uuid "7f2d3fee-96ee-53a9-be0e-e1fbe16389df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{62.32}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5f880ae-7e88-52b7-a206-237ab0ac051c" :answer/id #uuid "a5f880ae-7e88-52b7-a206-237ab0ac051c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{64.66}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" :answer/id #uuid "e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{75.31}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05d03192-075e-55e0-bdfc-5e2f0694c8cb" :answer/id #uuid "05d03192-075e-55e0-bdfc-5e2f0694c8cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{76.23}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fa9dd91-9cae-5db7-9b32-261b4c37d3a2" :answer/id #uuid "4fa9dd91-9cae-5db7-9b32-261b4c37d3a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{79.11}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9155dd9-42f9-5296-ab8b-8f9a62ed1901" :question/id #uuid "a9155dd9-42f9-5296-ab8b-8f9a62ed1901" :question/math-academy-id "q-74127" :question/problem "Let $A(4,-1,1,0)$, $B(4,2,1,-1)$, and $C(0,-5,3,2).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠BAC.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $v=\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $w=\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =b-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =c-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =m∠BAC$ be the angle between the vectors $v$ and $w.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{40}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14÷2}{20÷2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2479cbeb-b8bf-594f-bb65-af712cb46af1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2479cbeb-b8bf-594f-bb65-af712cb46af1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2479cbeb-b8bf-594f-bb65-af712cb46af1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f4e0ea9d-b064-574a-8020-d0183f158a87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96e7ebf1-4fb9-58fa-aebc-0738bb73140a" "answer-a9f16ae1-06fd-54bb-9718-edf7f92178cf" "answer-0b527f93-eccd-5722-9c2d-69b9b28ccd4c" "answer-f4e0ea9d-b064-574a-8020-d0183f158a87" "answer-9cd22dac-67da-5811-85d4-d003f4ac96bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96e7ebf1-4fb9-58fa-aebc-0738bb73140a" :answer/id #uuid "96e7ebf1-4fb9-58fa-aebc-0738bb73140a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9f16ae1-06fd-54bb-9718-edf7f92178cf" :answer/id #uuid "a9f16ae1-06fd-54bb-9718-edf7f92178cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b527f93-eccd-5722-9c2d-69b9b28ccd4c" :answer/id #uuid "0b527f93-eccd-5722-9c2d-69b9b28ccd4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4e0ea9d-b064-574a-8020-d0183f158a87" :answer/id #uuid "f4e0ea9d-b064-574a-8020-d0183f158a87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cd22dac-67da-5811-85d4-d003f4ac96bd" :answer/id #uuid "9cd22dac-67da-5811-85d4-d003f4ac96bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b814c1fe-b3ea-5814-8155-d4d3b5c1b767" :question/id #uuid "b814c1fe-b3ea-5814-8155-d4d3b5c1b767" :question/math-academy-id "q-74108" :question/problem "Let $A(2,2,1,-1)$ and $B(1,0,0,3).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2341e109-cf93-5cde-8264-a38a622dd7d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2341e109-cf93-5cde-8264-a38a622dd7d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2341e109-cf93-5cde-8264-a38a622dd7d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e698747a-e144-509c-878f-dd7465f64c4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e698747a-e144-509c-878f-dd7465f64c4b" "answer-bea321e3-d34c-528a-b52b-8d573230ecb2" "answer-72d013f0-3dae-5e02-a5be-5238527465e0" "answer-c20bf632-c8b7-5a89-921e-d119ea1ba35e" "answer-424982fb-be81-57d8-9104-2bf95d695266"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e698747a-e144-509c-878f-dd7465f64c4b" :answer/id #uuid "e698747a-e144-509c-878f-dd7465f64c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bea321e3-d34c-528a-b52b-8d573230ecb2" :answer/id #uuid "bea321e3-d34c-528a-b52b-8d573230ecb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72d013f0-3dae-5e02-a5be-5238527465e0" :answer/id #uuid "72d013f0-3dae-5e02-a5be-5238527465e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c20bf632-c8b7-5a89-921e-d119ea1ba35e" :answer/id #uuid "c20bf632-c8b7-5a89-921e-d119ea1ba35e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-424982fb-be81-57d8-9104-2bf95d695266" :answer/id #uuid "424982fb-be81-57d8-9104-2bf95d695266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135038 :tutorial/content "The **Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality** (sometimes called the **Cauchy-Bunyakovsky-Schwarz Inequality**) provides a connection between dot products and norms. It states that for any two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, we have the inequality\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the absolute value of the dot product is no more than the product of the norms.\n\nFor instance, if ![](images/6f/6f13203f5c0bc85b3af24b0a276e30044825c0133a6e8b42e6500cb20c0e754c.svg) and ![](images/80/809bfd2b9daeb6f8eee30bbe9ddbfe996326dcf1954cd3f9e09e072e95a16aa3.svg) then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |3\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot 12| & \\le \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\ |-33| & \\le 5\\cdot 13 \\\\ 33 & \\le 65.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An easy trick to remember the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is to think of the formula for the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nBecause $\\vert \\cos \\theta \\vert \\leq 1,$ we can see that\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The above trick is only a mnemonic trick, *not* an actual proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. The formula $\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta$ is actually a *consequence* of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, so a proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality cannot use the formula." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133988 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186053727 :answer-field/choices 17592186053728] [:db/retract 17592186053727 :answer-field/choices 17592186053729] [:db/retract 17592186053727 :answer-field/choices 17592186053731] [:db/retract 17592186053727 :answer-field/choices 17592186053732] {:db/id 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" "question-8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" "question-a06dc73c-225f-5c37-b7d5-563ea645e257" "question-85ebb17c-ed44-5cf2-a5c7-233470157f33"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054496 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{20}{8(z-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator  becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5,$ and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1).$\n\n![](images/47/47eb2277b9892fbd83cb1ae58c1c2a7640c29326402970254fb119c8c6c9dd5f.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n![](images/28/28f5bd692129118ef790ef1dde224e18f0a70674bf6ccd19eed4dbdb78b7b1f6.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054767 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4044453a-5a6d-5fd7-b8c8-665b004cb36e" "question-61eea428-2437-5847-999f-591f4a5a456c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054495 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](images/51/51bf9631f8b2ca90bda343a84a675f302ebae205446e528f5f05a8ea1f5f577b.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/f3/f3edb95bc0a0a57a6dc439a06e1653d95c23dcd49e701b4d4d3f3d8c0f36c594.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fad200a4-e2f6-57c9-aa77-2766fc482dc1" "question-d8919a5c-5e3b-5274-9d2f-901afddd5e90"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054590 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator  becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2),$ and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z.$\n\n![](images/70/70068644b054adae3915b30b4c8fe3745190ec2a9eef2b1672528189e8dab9fd.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/38/382b839130e9e8b59572303be4644e8c45c2be55c4b9f45ff2bd664037eecf8e.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054691 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-155136a2-7453-5b52-882f-507c2e2bad0d" "question-b735757b-2dac-511b-87bc-3911444d7136"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054591 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1),$ and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d19b8a64cafa7f78bdf3795eba734282ad0a158b26ce12978f474aa12c84211.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n![](images/72/72b3b315f67d06181cc1457b16fcee0e886c224960030a5975723761513e2371.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2a}{b}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" :question/id #uuid "b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-41549" :question/problem "$\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $16$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2,$ and the $20(y-3)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bb2dabd-d3ff-58f7-863a-fb9e1d9b390d" "answer-2e0cda42-eedf-5058-93d1-bf12ded0aa50" "answer-8b7d6847-74d4-52eb-a60f-8f439d660902" "answer-b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" "answer-90dafb0a-1f40-562d-bd76-33cd101d6a40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bb2dabd-d3ff-58f7-863a-fb9e1d9b390d" :answer/id #uuid "0bb2dabd-d3ff-58f7-863a-fb9e1d9b390d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5(y-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e0cda42-eedf-5058-93d1-bf12ded0aa50" :answer/id #uuid "2e0cda42-eedf-5058-93d1-bf12ded0aa50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(y-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b7d6847-74d4-52eb-a60f-8f439d660902" :answer/id #uuid "8b7d6847-74d4-52eb-a60f-8f439d660902" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5(y-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" :answer/id #uuid "b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90dafb0a-1f40-562d-bd76-33cd101d6a40" :answer/id #uuid "90dafb0a-1f40-562d-bd76-33cd101d6a40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}(y-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" :question/id #uuid "8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75370" :question/problem "$\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $18a$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a,$ and the $9(a+2)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot a}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot a}{a+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a}{a+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{2a}{a+2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82d46f26-d6b2-510f-a75b-7142f40cc7f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82d46f26-d6b2-510f-a75b-7142f40cc7f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "82d46f26-d6b2-510f-a75b-7142f40cc7f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c48f353-e4e8-5167-a7c6-69ea1c45a203" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb512587-b0fa-560f-b1c8-d8ffb99f24fd" "answer-efe9d29e-816e-5